2026-04-06 01:27:18 - Agent_20260406012402 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed 3 CodeQL queries with 29 total results across NiFi and NiFi Registry shell authorization components. Found a systemic OS command injection vulnerability pattern where user-controlled values (user identity, userId, groupId, groupName) from HTTP API requests flow through the authorization pipeline into shell commands executed via `sh -c`. 

Key findings submitted (9 total true positives):
1. NiFi ShellRunner (line 60) - the core execution sink using `ProcessBuilder(sh, -c, command)`
2. NiFi NssShellCommands - getGroupMembers(), getUserById(), getGroupById() embed user values into shell commands via String.format()
3. NiFi OsxShellCommands - same pattern for macOS with additional backtick injection risk in getGroupById()
4. NiFi RemoteShellCommands - user values embedded into SSH command strings passed to sh -c
5. NiFi Registry ShellRunner (line 60) - same sink pattern as NiFi
6. NiFi Registry NssShellCommands - same command building pattern
7. NiFi Registry OsxShellCommands - same macOS command building pattern

The ShellUserGroupProvider.getDelayProperty/getTimeoutProperty findings from Query 2 were determined to be false positives - these configuration values only appear in error messages, never in shell commands.

All vulnerabilities require authentication and the ShellUserGroupProvider to be configured (non-default), but still represent critical OS command injection risks.
